Make the standalone TiniSAT's seed reproducible and recordable.

As shipped, tinisat_standalone.cc seeds from the wall clock
(srandom(genRandomSeed())), so the seed can be neither reproduced nor recorded.
This patch reads TINISAT_SEED and seeds both srandom() and srand().

IMPORTANT -- this does NOT create a portfolio. In the tested configuration,
changing the seed did not alter the returned model or the observed trajectory
(seeds 1, 2 and 3 gave the byte-identical model). Keeping the seeded call is
harmless, but for real, reproducible diversification use a seeded clause-order
permutation (portfolio/engines/tinisat_perm.sh) or explicit cube assumptions
(portfolio/engines/tinisat_cube.sh).

Apply:   cd dagster/dagster && patch -p0 < /path/to/tinisat-seed.patch
         make -C standalone_tinisat

--- standalone_tinisat/tinisat_standalone.cc
+++ standalone_tinisat/tinisat_standalone.cc
@@ -68,7 +68,16 @@
   
   std::string str ("");
   clock_t tStart = clock();
-  srandom(genRandomSeed());
+  {
+    /* TINISAT_SEED makes a run reproducible; absent, fall back to the clock.
+       Seed BOTH generators: SatSolver.cpp uses rand(), not random(). */
+    const char *seed_env = getenv("TINISAT_SEED");
+    unsigned int seed = seed_env ? (unsigned int) strtoul(seed_env, NULL, 10)
+                                 : (unsigned int) genRandomSeed();
+    srandom(seed);
+    srand(seed);
+    fprintf(stderr, "c tinisat seed %u\n", seed);
+  }
   
   // load the CNF into new solver
   Cnf* cnf = new Cnf((const char*)argv[1]);
